[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I\'ve been fortunate enough to do a little over 150 AI coaching calls in the last 8 months. I\'ve worked with private equity firms, law firms, HVAC companies, all types of industries. And honestly, it\'s the same handful of mistakes over and over and over in every'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single call. The same five mistakes. Doesn\'t matter how big the company is. So, I figured I\'d walk you through all of those different mistakes and roughly in the order that they cost you. And the biggest one, the one that I see'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wreck the most time and money, it isn\'t prompting at all. We\'ll get to there in a second. We\'ll start with the first one. So, the first mistake is simple. It\'s blaming the AI when there\'s a simple setting to actually fix the issue. So, when people interact with AI, they'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'often get a bad output. And I\'ll give you a story. So, I work with a series of executive assistants that were working with AI. And one of the use cases was being able to extract information from invoices. And they were really upset about the fact that the invoice extraction wasn\'t'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working effectively for the AI. It kept on getting things wrong. And the first thing I do anytime I work with any client is ask them to share their screen with me. And what I do is I actually look specifically at the model and the reasoning effort. A lot of'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people don\'t know that there\'s multiple settings you can adjust here. You can choose the size of the brain and how hard that brain works. And that\'s what the intelligence is when you look at the different models you can choose from, as well as the effort level. Doesn\'t matter if you\'re in ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude.'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, that\'s the first mistake is making sure you\'re choosing the right model for the right task. And most data-heavy tasks where there\'s this extraction, you want to use a high-end model with a decent amount of effort. So, depending on the tool right now at this point in'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time, that might be GPT-5.6 with extra high reasoning or maybe Opus 5 with high effort. That\'s the first mistake. The second mistake is hoping that the AI will write like you without you training it on your'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'writing style. And this really comes down to the use case that most my clients get the most value from early on, which is having AI triage their inbox and respond on their behalf. Or at least draft responses on their behalf.'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But people often complain about the way the AI writes. They say it sounds like AI or it\'s AI slop. There\'s very simple way to fix this is by giving AI examples of your writing style in the past and having it create a literature fingerprint.'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And my common tactic I use to avoid this mistake of getting AI slop from AI when it writes in your behalf, especially for emails, is simply point the AI at your inbox via connector. So, ChatGPT and Claude both have these where you can connect your AI to Gmail, you can'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connect it to Outlook, whatever else. And simply have it look at your sent inbox and look at maybe the last three to 400 emails you\'ve sent and extract two things. First off, what are the types of emails that I\'m responding to,'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the categories of emails I respond to. After we have the type, we then figure out the fingerprint. So, for each one of those categories that I respond to, how do I write in that tone? So, what\'s the tone, what\'s the length, all that stuff. It\'ll figure out the fingerprint as well'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as the type of category, give that all back to you. Once you\'ve done that, you can then embed it into a skill and then you have an AI that can triage your inbox for you and then respond like you would for different types of emails. That\'s our second category.'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Our third category is automatically assuming that more context is better. So, you may want to give an AI 50, 100, or 800 files I\'ve seen. I\'ve actually worked with a few executives where they wanted to give an AI 800 files at once'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to make sure it had the right context. And this is often never the right option for most use cases. Instead of thinking more context is better, you should think the right context is better. And sure, when I say'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the right context, that might be 10 or 50 pages of context, but often times it\'s not. Often times it\'s maybe 10 to 20 to achieve a certain task at hand. And the reason we can\'t automatically assume that more context is better is'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you\'re working with these models, the way that they\'re set up is there\'s a certain amount of space in their head. And the more space you fill in the head of the AI, the dumber it gets. Especially if you go past around 50 to 60% of the space in the AI\'s head. And'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why that matters is when you fill the AI\'s head up with more data, there\'s less space in the AI\'s head for it to reason and think through the specific task at hand you\'ve given it. And that\'s our third mistake. Don\'t simply assume that more context is'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'better. Now onto our fourth mistake, and this one has a pretty big impact on a lot of people I work with. And it really comes down to data extraction tasks. So, a lot of us, we have data that we want the AI to pull information from. Once it pulls information from that data, we want it'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to then do some sort of analysis and then put it somewhere else. Either it it manifests into a report, a presentation, a decision, whatever else it is. And in this process, we\'re sometimes automatically defaulting to trusting the AI\'s numbers.'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And we should really avoid doing that. The reason being is there\'s three specific things we need to improve in the AI before we actually can trust those numbers. We have sourcing, citations, and incentives. Those are the three things. We\'ll start with sourcing. So, sourcing, when we give an AI'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'documents, we need to first make sure that it\'s actually getting information from these documents and nowhere else. Because the AI can get information from three places. They can get it from the documents I shared, they can get it from the internet, or they can get it from its own knowledge base. We don\'t want it'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from the internet, and we don\'t want it from its knowledge for most of these extraction tasks. Instead, we want it just from these documents. So, we need to ground the AI with a prompt saying that when I give you these documents, only base your information off of those and nothing else. That\'s'

[04:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the first thing. The next thing is citations. So, instead of simply trusting the AI, we want it to cite everything it\'s pulled from. So, when I give you the documents, I want it to tell me exactly what page, what section, what sentence you got that information from for every piece of data that you'

[05:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extract. So, I then can validate afterwards and verify that it\'s actually correct. And do it quickly because I know exactly where it came from. That\'s the second thing on citations. And then the third one is incentives.'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, with an AI, its sole purpose in life is to make you happy. And the way that it does that is by giving you answers. And what we need to do is we need to alter those incentives. So, we need to basically let the AI know that it\'s okay to say that you\'re not sure. So, if it\'s'

[05:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pulling information from a series of documents, we need to quickly let the AI know in a prompt before it actually does anything that if you can\'t find the information in the documents, just say that you don\'t know. And one little tactic I found with prompting is that when you tell the AI that if it gives'

[05:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you a wrong answer, it\'s actually 3x worse than telling me that you\'re unsure of the answer or telling me it\'s not found. And that alters the incentives. And that\'s our fourth mistake. Our fifth and final mistake is allowing the AI to build you apps when it shouldn\'t. So,'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you\'re working with Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, it doesn\'t matter. Many of these AIs, they want to build you prototypes. So, dashboards, things like that. And they look aesthetically pleasing. They look amazing sometimes. But the issue here is often times you\'re'

[06:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building an app when you could just use the AI features native to the tool. So, you could use Claude skills, GPT projects, GPT skills, it doesn\'t matter. And'

[06:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I couldn\'t tell you how many times I\'ve worked with clients where they\'ve built something and they thought, "Oh, this is really cool and it\'s really useful." And it is. But also, they can\'t share it easily. There\'s very little way for them to actually host the data for this'

[06:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'application, to host the code for it, and do all these other things because it\'s way too technical. And it tends to be over-engineering for the thing they\'re trying to solve. And it\'s not their fault. It\'s the AI\'s fault because the AI defaults to building apps instead of thinking about'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what\'s the simplest solution to solve this problem that\'s not just simple to solve the problem, but also easily shareable with everybody else on my team. So, they then can benefit from the work that I\'ve done. And that\'s why so often when I work with my clients, I'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'often transition them from having the AI build them an app after it\'s built it and transitioning that work over to a skill or a project so you then can bake that solution into something that\'s easily shareable for everybody else and'

[07:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it automatically works out of the box. And you don\'t have to deal with all the issues and the headaches that come with building apps. And that\'s our fifth and final mistake. So, as a quick recap of the five mistakes we talked through. So, the first thing that we mentioned'

[07:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was simply not blaming the AI on giving you poor outputs. You first want to change the two settings of making sure you have the right model with the right reasoning effort. The second thing we talked about is complaining that the AI sounds too robotic when it writes. And you complain'

[07:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without actually training the AI on your writing style. The third is assuming that giving the AI more context is always better. That\'s often not true. You want to give the right context at the right time. The fourth mistake is simply trusting the AI\'s numbers when it'

[07:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'goes to the data extraction process for you, pulling information from one place and putting it to another. And there are the three things that I mentioned about how you can fix that with citations, sourcing, and incentives. And then finally, the fifth mistake was'

[08:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'allowing the AI to build you apps when it shouldn\'t. It\'s over-engineering when you could simply just build something baked into the tools themselves, like skills or projects, easily share that with your teams, and propagate that benefit for everybody. So, if you'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enjoyed this, you\'ll likely enjoy two other things. First off, below is a 30-day AI insight series completely free. You\'ll get 30 insights in your inbox of how you can apply AI to your business and your work. The second thing is if you\'d like to'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work with me, below are a series of offerings to see if there\'s a good fit between the two of us. So, if you enjoyed this, you\'re likely going to love this video because the YouTube gods says that you\'ll enjoy it. So, you should give it a click and check it out. See you next time you\'re on here.'
